"Building a Solar System | VirtualDemos",
"Building a Solar System",
"<strong>Setups in the demo:</strong> Our solar nebula; Slow spin; Fast spin; No spin; Frost line, today’s Sun; Frost line, bright young Sun; Minimum mass disk; Three times heavier disk; Gas gone early; Early planetesimal melts; Late planetesimal stays solid; Small planetesimal stays solid; Vesta sized.",
"The demo tells the story in four stages, chosen along the top of the picture: {#} Collapse, {#} Frost line, {#} Planets grow and {#} Iron cores. Each setup opens its own stage. Every stage has a 3D view (drag to turn it) and a Flat view with graphs.",
"<strong>Collapse.</strong> A cloud of gas, shown as {#} parcels, falls toward the young Sun at its center. One parcel at the cloud’s edge, on its equator, is tracked with an orange ring. The readouts give its distance from the spin axis and how long it takes to go around once. The run ends when every parcel has landed in the disk or fallen onto the Sun; To the end skips ahead.",
"<strong>Frost line.</strong> The disk’s temperature at each distance from the Sun, and the solids that can form there. Drag the orange probe (or use its slider) to read the temperature at a distance. The Sun’s brightness can be changed.",
"<strong>Planets grow.</strong> {#} planetesimals spread through the disk join into bigger bodies over {#} million years. Brown bodies are rocky, blue ones are icy, and gold rings mark giant planets: cores that reached {#} Earth masses while the disk still had gas. The Flat view plots each body’s mass against its distance, with today’s planets as gray circles.",
"<strong>Iron cores.</strong> A planetesimal heated from inside by radioactive aluminum {#}, shown cut in half and colored by temperature. The graph follows the temperature at its center for {#} million years after the first solids formed in the disk.",
"Record reading stores the readouts of the stage you are in. Export to worksheet sends the readings to the table for that stage below; then Clear before you start the next stage.",
"Each stage is a simplified model, described under The model and its formulas in the demo’s Lab section. The models are real physics, but they leave a lot out, so the numbers are good to within a factor of about two, not exact.",
"Follow the steps that turned a cloud of gas and dust into our solar system, and use them to explain why the planets near the Sun are small and rocky while the ones far out are giants.",
"<strong>A spinning cloud.</strong> Stars and planets form inside cold clouds of gas and dust. When a dense part of a cloud starts to fall in on itself, it is always turning a little. As it shrinks it spins faster, just as a figure skater spins faster on pulling in their arms: the spin (called angular momentum) cannot be lost, so a smaller spinning object must turn faster. Gas that falls straight along the spin axis can reach the young Sun, but gas that falls from the sides cannot get closer than a certain distance before it is moving sideways fast enough to stay in orbit. Gas falling from above and below meets in the middle, so the cloud flattens into a spinning disk.",
"<strong>Hot near the Sun, cold far out.</strong> The young Sun heats the disk, and the temperature falls with distance. Each material turns from gas to solid at its own temperature, called its condensation temperature. Metal and rock condense even at over {#} K, but water freezes into ice only below about {#} K. The distance where the disk is cold enough for water ice is the <strong>frost line</strong> (or snow line). Beyond it, ice adds to the solids, and there is far more ice than rock in a gas of the Sun’s makeup.",
"<strong>Planetesimals and planets.</strong> Dust grains stick together into bodies called <strong>planetesimals</strong>, from about a kilometer up to hundreds of kilometers across. Their gravity pulls them together, and they join into larger bodies. A body grows until it has swept up the solids in a ring around its orbit; the mass it ends with is called its isolation mass. A core of about {#} Earth masses is heavy enough to pull in the hydrogen and helium gas around it and become a giant planet, but only if the gas is still there. Young stars lose their disks’ gas within a few million years.",
"<strong>Melting from inside.</strong> The first solids in the disk contained aluminum {#}, a radioactive kind of aluminum with a half life of about {#} million years. Its decay heats a planetesimal from inside. If the planetesimal formed early enough, while plenty of aluminum {#} was left, its inside could melt, and the heavy iron would sink to the center to form an iron core. Iron meteorites are pieces of such cores.",
"A cloud {#} AU across in radius shrinks into a disk about {#} AU in radius. Guess: does the gas at the disk’s edge turn faster than the cloud did by about {#} times, {#} times, {#} times or {#} million times?",
"Which do you expect to find closer to the Sun: rocky bodies or icy bodies? Why?",
"Aluminum {#} has a half life of {#} million years. What fraction of it is left after {#} million years? After {#} million years?",
"<strong>Part {#}: Collapse.</strong> Choose Our solar nebula, let the collapse finish and record a reading of the tracked parcel. Repeat with Slow spin and with Fast spin (the same cloud turning once every {#} and every {#} million years). Then watch No spin and note what happens. Send the three readings to the worksheet and clear them.",
